                    briefing_title_r1:      `Round 1 — Diagnose the mission–margin tension`,
                    briefing_text_r1:       `ICL's dean has handed you the flagship launch. The board's mandate is unforgiving: clear the <strong>MXN 18M</strong> budget <em>and</em> contribute <strong>MXN 6M</strong> of margin in the first cohort — while staying demonstrably faithful to ICL's humanistic mission. Diagnose where mission and margin genuinely <strong>conflict</strong> (the coaching-to-content ratio is the costliest values line) and where they <strong>reinforce</strong> each other (purpose-led buyers pay more). Arandia's lens says the mission is not a slogan but a set of costed design choices. State a design philosophy — and decide how seriously you treat the senior faculty who say they won't teach a “leadership-as-ROI bootcamp.”`,
                    briefing_title_r2:      `Round 2 — Choose the framing and set the price`,
                    briefing_text_r2:       `The first central decision. A <strong>humanistic/purpose</strong> framing draws a smaller, mission-aligned, higher-willingness-to-pay audience; an <strong>ROI/career-acceleration</strong> framing draws volume but commoditises you against the MX$70,000 online competitor and dilutes the mission. Set tuition (target <strong>MX$95,000</strong>) and a defensible cohort size against break-even (<em>~190</em>), the margin threshold (<em>~253</em>) and the 320 cap. Value-based pricing means purpose buyers may sustain a premium — but a framing must realistically support the size you forecast, and under-pricing won't clear the margin even with a full room.`,
                    briefing_title_r3:      `Round 3 — Allocate the budget and set the coaching ratio`,
                    briefing_text_r3:       `Allocate the full <strong>MXN 18M</strong> across modules &amp; content, executive coaching, experiential/retreat work, marketing and participant services. Then set the <strong>coaching-to-content ratio</strong> — the design variable that most embodies the humanistic commitment and most directly drives unit cost. Coaches cost <strong>MX$2,200/h</strong> versus content faculty at <strong>MX$1,400/h</strong>: promise everyone deep one-to-one coaching without costing it and the humanistic ambition <em>quietly blows up the P&amp;L</em>. Find the ratio that is both transformational and solvent — and fund enough marketing to actually fill the room.`,
                    briefing_title_r4:      `Round 4 — Defend the program to board and faculty`,
                    briefing_text_r4:       `The 15 May lock approaches. Pitch to the facilitator playing <strong>both</strong> the commercially-minded board and the mission-guardian senior faculty — two audiences with opposing fears. Defend that the program is simultaneously <strong>commercially viable</strong> and <strong>authentically humanistic</strong>: show the costed P&amp;L, hold the faculty by co-designing the values experience, and prove that what the program <em>does</em> matches what the framing <em>says</em>. Ignore the faculty and your differentiation walks out the door by September; let the design become a humanistic veneer over an ROI core and participants feel the gap on day one.`,

