                    board_dodge:            `Reframe away from money — argue mission matters more than the margin target this cohort`,
                    faculty_codesign:       `Show them the coaching ratio, experiential design and their named role — co-design the values experience with them`,
                    faculty_reassure:       `Reassure with mission language but keep the lecture-heavy design unchanged`,
                    faculty_ignore:         `Skip them — the board signs the budget; faculty can be replaced if they walk`,
                    coherence_aligned:      `Aligned — the humanistic framing is backed by real coaching, experiential work and faculty buy-in`,
                    coherence_partial:      `Partly — the language is strong but the design is thinner than the brochure implies`,
                    coherence_veneer:       `Veneer — humanistic words over an essentially ROI, lecture-driven design`,

                    coach_default:          `Hold both audiences at once: the board's MX$6M margin and the faculty's dignity test are the same design told two ways.`,
                    coach_r1_reinforce:     `That's Arandia's core claim: for ICL the mission IS the differentiation. Purpose-led buyers will pay the premium that makes a credibly humanistic design the commercially robust one — now you have to actually build it.`,
                    coach_r1_balance:       `A negotiated balance is honest, but watch the asymmetry: when margin is squeezed it's always the coaching and reflection that get trimmed first. Decide in advance what you will protect at all costs.`,
                    coach_r1_margin:        `Margin-first with mission as a wrapper is exactly the trap. If the humanistic language is bolted on afterwards, your senior faculty and corporate clients — who chose ICL for the mission — will feel the incoherence immediately.`,
                    coach_r1_mission:       `Mission at any cost is noble, but an institution that closes serves no one's dignity. Humanistic management doesn't mean financially unsustainable — find where purpose-led buyers let you protect the mission at a price they'll pay.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `Sharp diagnosis. The coaching-to-content ratio is precisely where mission and margin collide — and you've named the mission as the differentiation rather than a cost. That's the position that can hold both the board and the faculty.`,
                    coach_r1_volume:        `“Just fill the room to 320” makes the conflict disappear only on paper. Maximum volume at a commoditised price is exactly how you lose the willingness-to-pay premium that funds the humanistic design.`,
                    coach_r1_price:         `Cutting price for “accessibility” is a real value, but here it guarantees you miss the MX$6M mandate — and a program that can't fund a second cohort isn't accessible to anyone next year.`,
                    coach_r1_naive:         `“No conflict, they're automatically the same” is wishful. The whole exercise exists because deep coaching, retreats and small cohorts cost real money the board is watching. Name the trade-off so you can manage it.`,
                    coach_r1_lowfaculty:    `You've waved off the senior faculty. Their names and credibility are what make this “humanistic” rather than generic — treat them as a stakeholder now, or rediscover them as a crisis in Round 4.`,

                    coach_r2_valuepricing:  `Good value-based pricing: a premium under a purpose framing is exactly what purpose-led buyers will sustain. That's the counter-intuitive result — the mission-faithful design is the commercially stronger one.`,
                    coach_r2_underprice:    `You've priced near the MX$70,000 online competitor. Even a full room of 320 at that price can't clear the MX$6M margin — and you've signalled you're a commodity, not a transformation.`,
                    coach_r2_overprice:     `You've priced past what even purpose-led buyers will pay; the room thins out and your enrollment forecast won't hold. A premium is defensible only up to genuine willingness-to-pay.`,
                    coach_r2_roi:           `The ROI/bootcamp framing fills more seats but commoditises you against cheaper providers — and three senior professors just said they won't teach it. What are you now selling that the online competitor isn't?`,
                    coach_r2_humanistic:    `A pure humanistic framing protects the mission and the premium. Just make sure your cohort forecast is honest for the smaller, higher-WTP audience it draws — and that the price clears the margin.`,
                    coach_r2_hybrid:        `A deliberate hybrid — purpose narrative with an evidenced outcome — can hold the premium and the volume if you keep the design genuinely humanistic. The risk is letting it slide into veneer later.`,

                    coach_r3_balanced:      `A balanced coaching ratio that participants feel, costed against the margin — that's the transformational-but-solvent answer. The dignity experience is real and the P&L still works.`,
                    coach_r3_highcosted:    `Deep coaching, and you've funded it. Just confirm the margin still clears: high coaching is the most transformational and the most expensive design at once.`,
                    coach_r3_overrun:       `You promised a high coaching ratio without funding the hours. At MX$2,200 an hour across the cohort that's most of your budget — the humanistic ambition just quietly blew up the P&L. What's the ratio that's both transformational and solvent?`,
                    coach_r3_lowcoaching:   `A 10% coaching, lecture-heavy design under a humanistic brochure is the incoherence Arandia warns about: participants feel the gap on day one. Does your program DO what your framing SAYS?`,
                    coach_r3_nomarketing:   `You starved marketing — and a room that doesn't fill clears no margin no matter how beautiful the design. Some of your forecast cohort just evaporated.`,

                    coach_r4_board_evidence:`Leading the board with a costed P&L — enrollment, margin against MX$6M, and the premium the framing sustains — is exactly what earns the sign-off without over-promising.`,
                    coach_r4_board_promise: `Promising the numbers without the model invites the board to discount them. Show the P&L; trust is built on the enrollment-and-margin math, not on assurance.`,
